WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.760
Here is the first committed step drawn as simple beads on a string.

2
00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:12.060
Ribulose 1,5-bisphosphate is a five-carbon backbone capped at each end by a

3
00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:17.460
phosphate. The enzyme Rubisco grabs a single molecule of carbon dioxide and attaches

4
00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.220
it to that five-carbon chain, briefly making a six-carbon intermediate.

5
00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:25.560
That intermediate is unstable and immediately splits down the middle.

6
00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:30.620
The result is two identical three-carbon molecules of 3-phosphoglycerate.

7
00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:36.720
Notice the bookkeeping, one five-carbon sugar plus one carbon dioxide yields six

8
00:00:36.720 --> 00:00:40.020
carbons total, which divide cleanly into two threes.

9
00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:45.300
Counting carbons this way is the key to following the entire cycle without getting

10
00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:46.300
lost.

11
00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:50.140
Let us total the energy bill as a pyramid built from the bottom up.

12
00:00:50.620 --> 00:00:52.860
At the base, one carbon dioxide enters.

13
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:59.500
Three turns of the cycle, fixing three carbon dioxides, cost nine ATP and six NADPH,

14
00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:03.160
and yield one net molecule of glyceraldehyde 3-phosphate.

15
00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:08.420
To assemble a full six-carbon glucose, you must run the cycle six times, doubling

16
00:01:08.420 --> 00:01:12.800
everything. Eighteen ATP and twelve NADPH per glucose.

17
00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.180
Breaking it down by phase, fixation by Rubisco is energetically free.

18
00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:25.640
The reduction step that turns acid into sugar costs the ATP and NADPH, and

19
00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.340
regenerating ribulose bisphosphate costs additional ATP.

20
00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.400
Sugar is expensive, and this ledger shows exactly where every high-energy molecule

21
00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:35.400
is spent.

22
00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:39.060
Follow the dominoes that fall when a plant gets too hot.

23
00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:43.000
On a hot, dry day the plant closes its stomata to conserve water.

24
00:01:43.760 --> 00:01:48.540
Fresh carbon dioxide can no longer enter, and internal carbon dioxide drops while

25
00:01:48.540 --> 00:01:49.700
oxygen stays high.

26
00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:52.540
Heat also tilts Rubisco toward oxygen.

27
00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.880
Now oxygen starts winning the competition for Rubisco's active site and the enzyme

28
00:01:57.880 --> 00:01:59.560
misfires as an oxygenase.

29
00:02:00.160 --> 00:02:04.700
That wasteful oxygenase reaction burns carbon and energy for no sugar gained.

30
00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:09.320
Each step feeds the next and the chain ends in lost productivity.

31
00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:14.960
This cascade is a large part of why hot, dry climates are so hard on ordinary

32
00:02:14.960 --> 00:02:19.720
plants, and it sets up the two evolutionary fixes, spatial and temporal.

33
00:02:20.783 --> 00:02:24.923
Compare two runs of the cycle side by side, counting carbons the whole way.

34
00:02:25.603 --> 00:02:30.063
On top, the normal cycle, six carbon dioxides feed in, Rubisco makes twelve

35
00:02:30.063 --> 00:02:36.183
molecules of 3-PGA, which become twelve G3P, ten G3P regenerate the starting sugars,

36
00:02:36.523 --> 00:02:40.423
two exit, and you net one glucose, a gain of six fixed carbons.

37
00:02:41.003 --> 00:02:47.023
On the bottom, photorespiration, Rubisco grabs oxygen instead, producing 3-PGA plus

38
00:02:47.023 --> 00:02:52.143
phosphoglycolate. The salvage pathway recovers some carbon but releases carbon

39
00:02:52.143 --> 00:02:55.623
dioxide, and you actually lose three fixed carbons.

40
00:02:56.303 --> 00:03:00.763
If every reaction were oxygenation, too few carbons would remain to rebuild the

41
00:03:00.763 --> 00:03:05.723
starting sugar. Real leaves mix both, so net gain shrinks rather than stops.

42
00:03:06.443 --> 00:03:08.803
The contrast makes the cost unmistakable.
